WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.520
I went from sleeping on the streets of

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:07.600
Mumbai to becoming an MIT grad and then

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.800
building billion-dollar companies as a

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.800
CEO, investor and board member. And what

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.360
I learned was that the biggest advantage

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:18.400
isn't talent or techniques. It's

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.720
avoiding critical mistakes before they

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:23.039
cost you years and [music] your fortune.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:26.000
So, here are four principles behind

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:27.920
every real success story that help you

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.400
outpace everyone else [music] in

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:33.120
business and career. Principle number

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.920
one is learning to spot ghost towns

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.480
before you build in them. Years ago, my

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.399
CEO asked me if I could build a premium

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:43.200
consulting service that could help our

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:45.760
customers implement our tech platform.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:48.079
Now, we were selling into large

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.640
enterprise companies and it was a

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:54.719
complicated platform. So we hired

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:57.840
Wharton MBAs and MIT grads, XBCG

00:00:54.719 --> 00:01:00.480
consultants, solution architects, and we

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.640
spend months crafting the perfect pitch

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.840
deck, figuring out the implementation

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:06.240
processes [music]

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.640
and pricing model for the service. But

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:12.000
we had a little bit of problem while we

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:14.240
were building a perfect deck. Google had

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.720
quietly released a cheaper version of

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.920
that software and literally nuked the

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:23.119
market. We looked up and realized the

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:25.600
town was empty. We spent months building

00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:29.040
a Michelin star restaurant in a ghost

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:31.360
town. That failure taught me one very

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.360
expensive lesson. Intelligence is

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:36.479
knowing how to solve a problem. But

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:38.640
wisdom is knowing if that problem is

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:42.000
worth solving in the first place. And

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.920
the problem is for decades our education

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.399
system helps us live in these ghost

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:49.040
towns because universities train us for

00:01:46.399 --> 00:01:51.840
what? For convergence. They give you a

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.960
multiplechoice test where the answer is

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:57.920
already on the page. Follow the rules.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:00.799
Ace the test. Get a paper degree. The

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:03.520
system does not teach us to question the

00:02:00.799 --> 00:02:06.399
question, to question the professor. But

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:09.200
life is about divergence. Life gives you

00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:11.280
a blank sheet of paper. A Harvard

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:15.120
Business School professor studied

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:17.920
thousands of venturebacked startups. 75%

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.959
of those never returned a dollar to

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:24.480
investors. But here's the harsh part.

00:02:20.959 --> 00:02:26.800
48% or nearly half fail for just one

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.120
reason. There was no market need for

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:31.680
their solution. And it's not just about

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.239
business. We spend our careers living in

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:36.879
these ghost towns, too. So how do you

00:02:34.239 --> 00:02:39.120
avoid the ghost town in your career or

00:02:36.879 --> 00:02:42.879
in your business? Ask three questions.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:45.760
First, why am I doing this? Not how,

00:02:42.879 --> 00:02:48.560
what is your why? Second, whose life

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:51.840
actually changes if you do well? This is

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:54.879
a forcing function. And third, what

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.080
breaks if you don't do this? Be honest.

00:02:54.879 --> 00:02:58.800
Does anything break? [music]

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:01.280
If you're a founder or an operator,

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.760
you'll always feel the pressure. How do

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.920
I build this particular feature faster?

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.879
Or how do I optimize this particular

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:11.280
process? But the top 1% always start

00:03:08.879 --> 00:03:14.000
with a different question. Should this

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.400
exist at all? Don't optimize what you

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:19.040
can eliminate. Now, the second principle

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.760
is even more painful because it

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:24.959
questions the very advice you've heard

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.879
your entire life. Follow your passion.

00:03:24.959 --> 00:03:29.280
Haven't we all heard that romantic

00:03:26.879 --> 00:03:31.360
notion, do what you love and you'll

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.120
never work a day in your life? The only

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.519
people who say it, by the way, are

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.280
millionaires and movie stars who don't

00:03:35.519 --> 00:03:38.080
have to work a day in their lives

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.120
anyway. [music]

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:43.200
So, should you not have any passion? Of

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:46.239
course you should. What's life without

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:48.799
passion? The question is, can you turn

00:03:46.239 --> 00:03:51.360
your passion into a career [music] or a

00:03:48.799 --> 00:03:53.519
business? Because your passion is an

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.159
incredible fuel, but it's a terrible

00:03:53.519 --> 00:03:58.080
compass. It gives you the energy to move

00:03:56.159 --> 00:04:00.080
forward, but it's very bad at telling

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:03.200
you where to drive. Here's the

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:05.760
inconvenient truth. Look at any passion

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.159
industry, and you'll see a graveyard of

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:11.040
dreams. In Los Angeles and in New York

00:04:08.159 --> 00:04:13.487
City, thousands of talented dreamers

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.439
wait tables at midnight. In music,

00:04:13.487 --> 00:04:17.440
[music] in film, in fashion, on

00:04:15.439 --> 00:04:20.000
Broadway. Some call it the glamour

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.560
industry. I call it the glamour tax.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.800
It's the price you pay to be discovered.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:27.680
The supply of passionate talent is

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:30.720
infinite and the demand is so very

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:33.360
limited. So your value collapses to

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:35.103
zero. And I've seen often that when you

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.680
turn your talent into transaction,

00:04:35.103 --> 00:04:38.479
[music]

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.400
you lose the joy of both. But what about

00:04:38.479 --> 00:04:42.400
all these inspiring stories we hear

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.840
about passionate leaders who followed

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:45.759
their dreams? [music] You know, Bill

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:47.199
Gates falling in love with coding and

00:04:45.759 --> 00:04:49.600
dropping out of school and starting

00:04:47.199 --> 00:04:51.600
Microsoft. What gets left out of those

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:54.639
legends is the hidden advantage they

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:57.360
had. Bill Gay's father was a powerful

00:04:54.639 --> 00:05:00.160
lawyer with deep pockets and even deeper

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:02.400
network. Bill had no existential risk

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:04.560
when he dropped out of school and Bill's

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.800
mother served on a charity board with

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.360
the CEO of IBM and she was the one who

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:12.000
introduced Bill to the CEO. That's how

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:13.919
Microsoft got the deal of the century.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.320
None of that had anything to do with

00:05:13.919 --> 00:05:17.039
passion. Of course, Bill had passion for

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.600
coding, [music]

00:05:17.039 --> 00:05:22.400
but so did thousands of other kids. only

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:24.800
one of them had a hidden advantage. So

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:27.440
passion is great so long as there is a

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.919
market for it. I like what Mark Cuban

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:32.880
says, "Don't follow your passion, follow

00:05:29.919 --> 00:05:35.039
your efforts." Effort reveals what

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:38.000
you're willing to suffer for. Thousands

00:05:35.039 --> 00:05:40.720
of hours of painstaking effort builds

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:43.759
mastery. And the truth is, we love what

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:45.919
we're good at. So what's the action item

00:05:43.759 --> 00:05:48.720
here? [music] Before you build or chase

00:05:45.919 --> 00:05:51.280
anything, qualify it. Don't just ask if

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:53.759
you have passion for it. Run a test with

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:57.919
three questions. I call that framework

00:05:53.759 --> 00:06:00.800
lit. Leverage, insight, timing. It's a

00:05:57.919 --> 00:06:04.000
way to qualify good ideas, sustainable

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:06.880
ideas very quickly. L is for leverage.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.080
What unfair advantage can you start with

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:12.160
that others can't copy? Example is Bill

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:13.919
Gates. We talked about him already, but

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:16.240
there are people like Sam Alman, for

00:06:13.919 --> 00:06:18.960
example, or Adam Newman. They [music]

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:21.360
had deep networks and they were able to

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:24.080
raise billions of dollars even when

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:25.919
their businesses showed no hope of being

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:27.840
profitable [music] for years to come.

00:06:25.919 --> 00:06:30.160
But that was their unfair advantage.

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:33.680
They could outspend rivals before the

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:37.199
race even began. I stands for insight.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:38.960
What is that one secret thing about the

00:06:37.199 --> 00:06:40.960
market that [music] you know that

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:43.360
everybody else is missing? The example

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:46.080
is Larry Page. He realized that the

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:48.400
inbound links to any page were in fact

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:50.479
votes. Ranking the page on search

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.240
results by counting [music] those votes

00:06:50.479 --> 00:06:55.280
mattered more than counting the

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:57.600
keywords. That one insight built Google.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:59.599
Now Larry Page had no passion for

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:01.840
scaling Google. In fact, he wanted to

00:06:59.599 --> 00:07:06.240
sell it to Yahoo so he could go back to

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:08.880
his PhD program. And T is timing. Why

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:11.520
right now is [music] the only time your

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:14.160
idea works. Jeff Bezos didn't have a

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:16.880
deep passion for books either. He saw a

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:19.199
statistic that the internet was growing

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:21.680
at 2300%

00:07:19.199 --> 00:07:23.759
a year. He didn't follow his heart. He

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:26.000
followed the math. If you don't have one

00:07:23.759 --> 00:07:28.720
of the three advantages, leverage,

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:30.645
insight, or timing, you don't have a

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:33.199
business. You may have a romantic

00:07:30.645 --> 00:07:35.919
[music] delusion disguised as a dream.

00:07:33.199 --> 00:07:38.319
Now once you have avoided ghost towns

00:07:35.919 --> 00:07:40.800
and the passion graveyards, [music] you

00:07:38.319 --> 00:07:43.599
need to understand the DNA of your

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:45.680
business. Most of the time burnout

00:07:43.599 --> 00:07:47.680
doesn't come from working too hard. It

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:50.240
comes from working on the wrong thing.

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:52.880
You know, you can't make an elephant run

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:56.000
like a cheetah by feeding it cheetah's

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:58.479
diet. One is built for sheer power, the

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:00.639
other for sheer speed. All philosophers

00:07:58.479 --> 00:08:03.360
in the world have had the same insight.

00:08:00.639 --> 00:08:06.160
Know thyself. Know your nature. Every

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:09.759
business on earth is born with one of

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:12.400
the seven DNA signatures. That code

00:08:09.759 --> 00:08:14.960
determines what you can sell, what traps

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:17.919
you have to avoid, and how you win.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:21.120
First, service DNA. Here, what you sell

00:08:17.919 --> 00:08:23.599
is time and talent. The trap, well, the

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.840
time wall. Scale is all about how much

00:08:23.599 --> 00:08:28.639
of your time you can sell. If you're a

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:30.560
lawyer or an IT consultant, you stop

00:08:28.639 --> 00:08:32.800
working, the revenue stops coming.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:35.039
Scaling requires hiring more humans

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:37.200
which eats into your margin. So that's

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:40.159
the basic trap. And how do you win when

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:42.880
you have this DNA? You productize. Turn

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:45.279
your service into a repeatable playbook

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:48.480
that runs without you that others can

00:08:45.279 --> 00:08:50.959
run. Second physical product DNA. What

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.760
you sell here is physical goods. The

00:08:50.959 --> 00:08:56.720
world of atoms. The trap here is all

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:58.560
about cash. Your money is always locked

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:00.720
in inventory. You know, because you have

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:02.560
to pay for the goods before you can sell

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.040
them. So, how do you win when you have

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:07.600
this DNA? You have to invest in supply

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:10.480
chain and lean flow. You have to find

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:13.040
ways to let your market fund your

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:15.600
working capital. Third is the digital

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:17.600
product DNA. You sell digital goods. You

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:20.000
know, the world of bits and bites, the

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:23.120
software industry. The usual trap here

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:25.040
is called the J curve. is the cash burn

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:28.000
because the software business typically

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:30.640
requires massive upfront cash [music] in

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:32.800
R&D to build a platform with zero

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:34.480
revenue for months and that's why

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:36.240
there's [music] an entire venture

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:39.200
capital industry that funds these

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:41.279
companies during that phase. Now AI is

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:42.959
changing some of those dynamics but

00:09:41.279 --> 00:09:44.800
we'll talk about that in the coming

00:09:42.959 --> 00:09:48.240
episodes. How do you win in software

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:50.800
industry? Build the absolute minimum

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:52.880
product that you can to validate the

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:55.279
market first. If it's not fitting,

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:58.160
evolve it, change it, keep testing, keep

00:09:55.279 --> 00:10:00.880
trying. But before you take on massive

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:03.200
upfront cost, convince yourself [music]

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:05.519
that you have customers and they're

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:07.279
willing to pay for it. Fourth DNA

00:10:05.519 --> 00:10:09.839
signature is the marketplace [music]

00:10:07.279 --> 00:10:13.760
DNA. Here you are the matchmaker,

00:10:09.839 --> 00:10:16.399
matching supply with demand. The trap of

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:19.600
this business is perhaps the trickiest

00:10:16.399 --> 00:10:22.000
one. It's the two-sided hustle, right?

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:24.399
No buyers without sellers, no sellers

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:27.279
without buyers. And when you have this

00:10:24.399 --> 00:10:30.720
DNA signature, one way to win is to stay

00:10:27.279 --> 00:10:33.680
hyperfocused. Focus on dominating one

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:36.480
tiny niche to create liquidity first.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:39.440
Fifth, media DNA. What you're selling

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:42.399
here is human attention. The trap here

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:45.519
is the danger of the treadmill. Audience

00:10:42.399 --> 00:10:47.839
attention is a flimsy artifact. It's

00:10:45.519 --> 00:10:49.839
radioactive. It starts decaying the

00:10:47.839 --> 00:10:52.000
moment you stop demanding it or

00:10:49.839 --> 00:10:54.240
deserving it. So you have to keep

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:57.120
running just to stay in one place. So

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:59.360
how do you win here? One way is to build

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:02.320
direct relationship with your audience.

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:04.720
So move your audience from a rented

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:07.839
platform like social media to a direct

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:11.279
community like newsletters. Sixth,

00:11:07.839 --> 00:11:14.240
capital DNA. And this is the business of

00:11:11.279 --> 00:11:17.920
money, right? You're transacting on risk

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:20.240
and on yield. Banks, hedge funds,

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:23.360
insurance companies. The trap usually is

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:25.680
the concentration. One bad investment

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:28.240
can wipe out the entire fund. And then

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:30.720
the way to win here is diversification

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:33.360
and syndication of risk. That's why all

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.600
these capital businesses know a lot

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:38.160
about spreading their risk across a

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:40.959
network of partners instead of betting

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:43.839
the whole house on a single cart. And

00:11:40.959 --> 00:11:47.360
finally, the seventh signature assets

00:11:43.839 --> 00:11:50.480
DNA. This is where you sell access to a

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:53.120
location or an asset. Real estate, data

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:55.839
centers, those fit into this DNA. The

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:58.240
trap here is that you have to take on

00:11:55.839 --> 00:12:00.079
massive debt just to get started. But

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:03.519
the good thing is in these type of

00:12:00.079 --> 00:12:06.720
businesses your difficulty is your best

00:12:03.519 --> 00:12:09.279
defense because it costs $100 million to

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:11.120
build a data center. But then very few

00:12:09.279 --> 00:12:13.120
people can do it anyway, right? So once

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:15.920
you build it, you can extract monopoly

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:19.440
rent for years to come. So those are the

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:22.480
seven DNA signatures. Now most burnout

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:24.160
comes from the DNA mismatch. So for

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:26.000
instance, if you want a business that

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:27.839
gives you passive income while you

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:29.920
sleep, then you shouldn't start a

00:12:27.839 --> 00:12:31.920
services business, you should explore

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:34.160
something like the capital DNA where

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:36.800
your money is making money. Another

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:39.600
example, if you wanted to build an asset

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:42.399
light business with near zero marginal

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:44.959
cost, then don't start a physical

00:12:42.399 --> 00:12:47.920
product DNA business, right? Look at the

00:12:44.959 --> 00:12:50.560
digital DNA. So, make sure your goals

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:52.160
are matching the DNA signature of the

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:54.639
business you want to start. So, what's

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:57.680
the action item? It's a simple two-step

00:12:54.639 --> 00:13:00.720
process. First, examine the DNA of the

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:03.440
business you're in as an employer or as

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:05.360
an entrepreneur. Which signature does

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:07.839
your business have? And more [music]

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:10.800
importantly, is it capable of taking you

00:13:07.839 --> 00:13:13.120
where you want to go? And second, if you

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:15.279
are in the right place, then apply the

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:17.360
winning strategies. Choose the DNA

00:13:15.279 --> 00:13:19.519
signature that rewards the life you

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:21.839
actually want. Now, once you have chosen

00:13:19.519 --> 00:13:24.079
your DNA, you have one final problem.

00:13:21.839 --> 00:13:26.480
The fourth principle is what I call the

00:13:24.079 --> 00:13:28.639
high walls, and it's the secret weapon

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:29.920
to beat your competition. Winners don't

00:13:28.639 --> 00:13:32.160
win because they're better than

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:34.560
everybody else. They win because they do

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:37.920
something different. Peter Theal, the

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:40.880
co-founder of PayPal and investor in

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:43.440
Facebook and SpaceX and Airbnb and many

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:45.600
other companies, captures this idea

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:48.639
without mercy. He [music] says

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:50.880
competition is for losers. And what he

00:13:48.639 --> 00:13:53.279
means to say is this, that if you're in

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:56.160
a fair fight, you've already lost. The

00:13:53.279 --> 00:13:58.399
goal of your career or your startup is

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:59.440
not to compete. It's to make the

00:13:58.399 --> 00:14:01.680
competition [music]

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:03.760
irrelevant. Easier said than done,

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:05.839
right? How do you protect your fortress

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:08.560
so your rivals can't breach it? Build

00:14:05.839 --> 00:14:11.760
strong, high walls. There are three

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:14.000
specific ideas that once built make it

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:16.240
mathematically impossible for others to

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:18.800
breach your castle. Wall number one,

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:21.519
economies of scale. This is the cost

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:23.600
wall. Most businesses get more expensive

00:14:21.519 --> 00:14:25.920
to run as they get bigger. But a

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:29.360
business that has economies of scale

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:31.360
gets cheaper to run as it grows. Why?

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:33.125
Because the bigger you get, the lower

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:36.079
your cost of input and operations.

00:14:33.125 --> 00:14:39.120
[music] Let's say Walmart sells 10,000

00:14:36.079 --> 00:14:41.040
bottles of Tide detergent every day.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:43.519
When they negotiate with Proctor and

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:46.079
Gamble, they're going to say, "Hey, we

00:14:43.519 --> 00:14:49.360
want this for $4, [music] not six." And

00:14:46.079 --> 00:14:51.920
PNG has to say yes. Why? Because losing

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:55.199
Walmart would mean losing 3.6 million

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:58.240
bottles a year. If PNG says no, they

00:14:55.199 --> 00:15:01.199
lose the biggest shelf on Earth. This is

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:03.440
the cost wall. Wall number two, network

00:15:01.199 --> 00:15:06.160
effects. The most successful products

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:08.720
are those where every new user adds

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:11.760
value to every existing user. And the

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:14.240
party goes on. Think about Visa, the

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:16.639
credit card company. They have 4 billion

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:19.519
card holders and 100 million merchants

00:15:16.639 --> 00:15:22.160
who accept it worldwide. So more card

00:15:19.519 --> 00:15:24.560
holders make merchants want to accept

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:27.120
Visa more. And the more merchants accept

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:30.800
Visa, the more card holders want to use

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:34.160
it. Each site reinforces the other. Now

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:37.040
imagine I launch a rival credit card

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:39.199
called Lisa. Tomorrow with superior

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:42.240
technology, lower fees. I'll still have

00:15:39.199 --> 00:15:45.199
zero merchants and zero card holders.

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:47.519
Nobody wants a Lisa card from me because

00:15:45.199 --> 00:15:49.440
nobody accepts it. And nobody accepts it

00:15:47.519 --> 00:15:51.440
because nobody wants to use it. I'm

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:53.759
locked out. That's the power of network

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:56.800
effects. Wall number three, switching

00:15:53.759 --> 00:15:59.680
cost. This reminds me of that epic rock

00:15:56.800 --> 00:16:01.279
song, Hotel California from Eagles. You

00:15:59.680 --> 00:16:03.600
probably remember the final line from

00:16:01.279 --> 00:16:06.000
this song. You can check out anytime you

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:07.199
like, but you can never leave. They

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:09.199
probably didn't know, but they were

00:16:07.199 --> 00:16:11.519
talking about switching costs. Now, some

00:16:09.199 --> 00:16:13.680
switching costs are emotional. Apple's

00:16:11.519 --> 00:16:15.920
branding, for example. It makes you feel

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:18.240
attached to it. Even if someone will

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:21.360
tell you 10 things that Android phones

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:23.600
can do better, in that case better

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:26.240
doesn't matter and some switching costs

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:28.720
are the exact opposite. Take Salesforce.

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:32.320
It's a CRM platform for businesses.

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:34.880
Nobody loves Salesforce. However,

00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:37.120
ripping that software out and replacing

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:39.279
it with a rival would be a massive

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:41.680
headache. It would require migrating 10

00:16:39.279 --> 00:16:45.199
years of data, retraining 500

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:47.519
salespeople. The pain of leaving is way

00:16:45.199 --> 00:16:49.920
higher than the pain of staying. That's

00:16:47.519 --> 00:16:52.959
the final wall that your rivals can't

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:55.040
jump. So now the real question is, well,

00:16:52.959 --> 00:16:58.560
how do you build those walls? And the

00:16:55.040 --> 00:17:01.279
answer is surprisingly counterintuitive.

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:04.240
Start very small. You cannot have

00:17:01.279 --> 00:17:06.319
economies of scale or network effects on

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:08.959
day [music] one. If you try to capture

00:17:06.319 --> 00:17:10.640
1% of a hundred billion dollar market,

00:17:08.959 --> 00:17:13.360
you'll have competitors attacking you

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:16.640
from every side and direction. Instead,

00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:18.559
you should try and capture 70% of an

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:20.799
extremely small market that no one cares

00:17:18.559 --> 00:17:23.520
about. The examples are everywhere. For

00:17:20.799 --> 00:17:25.839
instance, Amazon didn't start as the

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:28.400
everything store. They monopolized very

00:17:25.839 --> 00:17:30.799
small market books. Facebook didn't

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:33.919
start as the world social network. They

00:17:30.799 --> 00:17:35.919
monopolized just one university first,

00:17:33.919 --> 00:17:38.880
Harvard. Walmart didn't start as

00:17:35.919 --> 00:17:43.039
America's retailer. They monopolized

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:46.000
Rogers Arkansas. A town so small Sears

00:17:43.039 --> 00:17:48.000
and Kmart would never bother to compete

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:50.960
there. And those two companies are gone.

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:54.400
Walmart is now a behemoth. So don't try

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:57.360
to build a wall around the entire world.

00:17:54.400 --> 00:18:00.320
Find a tiny [music] corner and build an

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:02.640
impenetrable wall around it. So, those

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:06.000
are the four pillars of our foundation.

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:07.360
Avoid ghost towns, avoid graveyards,

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:10.160
learn to read the DNA [music]

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:12.720
signatures, and build strong walls. But

00:18:10.160 --> 00:18:15.360
now comes the hard part. The messy,

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:18.080
unglamorous work of constructing your

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:20.559
masterpiece on top of this foundation.

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:23.200
How do you get your first customer when

00:18:20.559 --> 00:18:26.240
nobody knows you and no one trusts you?

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:29.280
How do you hire a players when you can't

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:30.559
afford them? How do you avoid burnout?

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:33.360
This is where you have to remind

00:18:30.559 --> 00:18:35.280
yourself that you are the builder, not

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:37.520
the building. Your business or your

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:40.320
career may rise, it may [music] fall,

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:41.840
but your foundation remains so you can

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:44.640
keep building. And that's where the

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:48.080
rubber meets the road. But that is our

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:50.640
episode two. And that comes next month.

